$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The case</h2>
<p>Meena married Sanjiv Saxena on 30 November 2001. On 19 April 2002 — four and a half months later — she was found hanging in the matrimonial home in Uttam Nagar. Her father\'s case was of relentless dowry pressure compressed into that short marriage: a demand of Rs 50,000 at the pheras of which Rs 42,000 was paid, a motorcycle, gifts demanded at Lohri and Holi, and money for repairs to the matrimonial house. The trial court convicted the husband and his family under Sections 498-A and 304-B read with 34 IPC in July 2004, sentencing them to imprisonment for life for the dowry death, while acquitting them of criminal breach of trust under Section 406. The appeal was filed the same year. It has taken twenty-two years to be heard and decided.</p>

<h2>The four ingredients, and the presumption</h2>
<p>The Division Bench worked through the classic structure of a Section 304-B case. Death by hanging within seven years of marriage was undisputed and unnatural. The evidence of the parents and relatives established cruelty and harassment; the demands they described were dowry demands within Section 2 of the Dowry Prohibition Act; and the harassment was proximate — soon before death — in the sense the section requires. With those four ingredients in place, Section 113-B of the Evidence Act did its statutory work: the court must presume that the accused caused the dowry death. The defence theory — suicide out of frustration that her parents did not visit — offered no credible displacement of that presumption, and the convictions followed. The corresponding provisions today are Section 80 of the BNS and the dowry-death presumption carried into the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam.</p>

<h2>The attack on the investigation</h2>
<p>The defence pressed the kind of arguments that two decades of hindsight make available: the earliest statements recorded by the SDM lacked the details that later emerged; witnesses improved their accounts; the investigating officer used a scribe for the case diary. The Bench\'s answers are of general utility. Statements to the police during investigation are not substantive evidence, and omissions count only when the witness is properly confronted — here the defence confronted witnesses with their SDM statements but not with the later supplementary statements. Early statements made on the day of the death could not realistically be expected to be exhaustive, given the family\'s shock. And there is no statutory bar on an officer taking a scribe\'s assistance for the case diary, absent demonstrated prejudice.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment is a reminder that in dowry death trials the battleground is almost always the quality of the family\'s testimony about demands and proximity. Investigative imperfections, standing alone, rarely dislodge a case whose core survives cross-examination — particularly where the statutory presumption has been triggered and the defence offers no affirmative explanation of the death.</p>
</div>

<h2>Pendency, bail and the end of the road</h2>
<p>Perhaps the starkest feature of the case is temporal. The appellants obtained suspension of sentence and spent the pendency on bail; one died while the appeal awaited hearing, and his appeal abated. For the survivors, the dismissal converts a 2004 sentence into a 2026 surrender direction — two weeks to present themselves before the trial court to serve the remainder of life imprisonment. Long pendency is sometimes assumed to soften outcomes. This judgment shows the opposite discipline: the appellate court decided the case on the record as it stood, and the passage of time earned the appellants neither acquittal nor leniency.</p>

<p>For families on either side of a Section 304-B case in Delhi, the practical lessons are constant across the decades: the prosecution case is built — or broken — in the first statements, the proximity evidence, and the confrontation of witnesses; and an appeal, however long it lasts, is argued on that record alone.</p>
